The rule pack
Deterministic validation of shape and order. Same input, same verdict, every time, no exceptions, no authority. The baseline pack:

- at least one step;
- known stages only;
- non-empty titles;
- review before the first assemble when both are present;
- verify is the final step when present.

A caller can swap in a stricter pack. It cannot swap in a looser one that outputs `ok` for plans these rules reject — the baseline is a floor, and the gate consults the pack it was given, whose rules are on its face.

Shape
```
proposal { id, objective, steps[], digest }
step     { stage, title }
stage    one of: research, work, review, assemble, verify
```

Config
Bloom reads `config.json` from its data directory, `~/.bloom` unless you pass `--data-dir`. Create the file yourself in a text editor, readable only by your account, and keep it out of every repository:

```json
{
  "version": 1,
  "providers": {
    "lab": { "kind": "chat-completions", "baseURL": "<your-endpoint>", "locality": "local" }
  },
  "models": {
    "aliases": [{ "alias": "coder", "backendModel": "<your model ID>", "workload": "code" }],
    "default": "coder"
  }
}
```

The provider name, here `lab`, is how Bloom refers to the provider in its output. A server on your private network needs `"locality": "local"`, and a plain HTTP server also needs `"allowInsecureHttp": true`. If your server requires a key, the README's Terminal commands section names the provider field that holds the name of the environment variable your shell keeps the key in. Put the key only in that variable, never in the file, a flag or the chat.

Check the file without sending anything to the server:

```bash
cd bloom && node bin/bloom.mjs doctor
```

The Endpoint rule row should read `pass` and name the provider and its locality.

Family conventions (observed, not invented)
These pairings are observed usage across the family, noted here so packs read consistently; they are conventions, not grammar:

- the `delete` verb pairs with the `destructive` effect (the memory fabric's note faces do this);
- retrieval faces use `read`, `list`, and `search` under the `read` effect;
- `credential` effects route the owner's decision at run time, per seed's reading classes.

Shape
```
openContinuity()                                     → frozen { continuities: [] }
lookupContinuity(ledger, { subject })                → frozen { attached, continuityId }
attachContinuity(ledger, { subject, continuityId? }) → frozen { ledger, continuityId, created }
isolateContinuity(ledger, { subject })               → frozen { ledger, continuityId, isolatedId }

continuity   frozen { id, subject, state }   state: active | isolated
```

`subject` is the non-empty string consent already uses for the subject of memory. A
continuity id is an opaque string, opened at random. Callers keep it and pass it back;
they do not build one.

Shape
```
store { epoch: number, records: [...] }   — epoch is monotonic, starts at 0
store { epoch, records, tombstones, receipts }
admitMemory(store, consentState, record)   → { store, admitted, reason? }   record may name `supersedes`
forgetMemory(store, { id, confirmed, key }) → { store, status, reason?, forgotten, erased?, receipt?, tombstones? }
projectionStamp(store)                     → store.epoch
projectionIsValid(store, stamp)            → stamp === store.epoch
buildProjection(store)                     → frozen { stamp, records }
readProjection(store, projection)          → { status: 'current' | 'refused', reason?, records, stamp, epoch }
recallCurrent(store, projection?)          → { projection, records, rebuilt }
```

Recorded conformance fixtures
`test/fixtures/provider-conformance.json` holds recorded request and response cases with
synthetic values. `test/provider-conformance.test.mjs` replays each case through a recorded
adapter, `validateProvider`, and `normalizeGeneration` with `fetch` disabled, and checks:

- valid completions with full, partial, and unreported usage (`null`, never `0`);
- malformed records (missing `text`, non-boolean `complete`, unknown usage field, negative or
  fractional counts, non-string `model`) fail closed with the named violation;
- truncated completions keep `complete: false`;
- a slow stream aborted by `signal` resolves with the partial text, `complete: false`, and
  `usage: null`, and the same stream left to finish earns `complete: true`;
- an unavailable model fails closed, and no other model is named in its place.

The fixtures name no address, host, or credential, and a test holds that.

Manifest shape
```json
{
  "version": 1,
  "generatedAt": "2026-11-22T10:00:00.000Z",
  "sources": {
    "seed": {
      "name": "seed",
      "head": "head sha of main",
      "syncedAt": "2026-11-22T10:00:00.000Z",
      "files": [{ "path": "README.md", "sha256": "64-hex digest", "bytes": 3150 }]
    }
  }
}
```

`generatedAt` moves only on runs that changed something. A source's `syncedAt` marks the last run that copied it, and does not march forward on runs that skipped it.
